==Season summary==
===Pre-season===
In the aftermath of the [[2022–23 Inter Milan season|2022–23 season]], Inter was forced to undergo an extensive squad makeover, as [[Samir Handanović]], [[Alex Cordaz]], [[Milan Škriniar]], [[Dalbert (footballer)|Dalbert]], [[Stefan de Vrij]], [[Danilo D'Ambrosio]], [[Roberto Gagliardini]] and [[Edin Džeko]] had their contracts set to expire,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://gianlucadimarzio.com/it/inter-calciomercato-calciatori-scadenza-contratto-2023|title=Inter: i giocatori in scadenza di contratto|date=25 February 2023|access-date=12 November 2023|language=it|publisher=Gianluca Di Marzio}}</ref> alongside [[Romelu Lukaku]], [[Francesco Acerbi]] and [[Raoul Bellanova]] having their loan spells coming to an end.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Calciomercato/Inter/08-12-2022/inter-nodo-prestiti-lukaku-acerbi-bellanova-asllani-4501220726817.shtml|title=Lukaku, Acerbi, Bellanova: voglia di conferme, i costi frenano i nerazzurri|date=8 December 2022|access-date=12 November 2023|language=it|publisher=[[La Gazzetta dello Sport]]}}</ref> On 1 July, Inter announced their first signing of the season, French striker [[Marcus Thuram]] from [[Borussia Mönchengladbach]], who agreed to a five-year contract with the ''Nerazzurri''.<ref name=thuram>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/marcus-thuram-signs-for-inter|title=Marcus Thuram signs for Inter|date=1 July 2023|access-date=1 July 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 3 July, Croatian playmaker [[Marcelo Brozović]] joined Saudi club [[Al Nassr FC|Al Nassr]] for a reported fee of €18 million, leaving Inter after eight and a half seasons.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://sport.sky.it/calciomercato/2023/07/03/brozovic-al-nassr-inter-calciomercato-news|title=Brozovic, ufficiale all'Al-Nassr: l'annuncio sui social. "Tranquilli, sono qui"|date=3 July 2023|access-date=12 November 2023|language=it|publisher=[[Sky Sport (Italy)]]}}</ref> On 5 July, both [[Alessandro Bastoni]] and [[Hakan Çalhanoğlu]] renewed their contracts, respectively until 2028 and 2027.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/alessandro-bastoni-inter-contract-extension-2028|title=Inter and Alessandro Bastoni together until 2028|date=5 July 2023|access-date=17 August 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/hakan-calhanoglu-inter-contract-extension-2027|title=Inter and Hakan Calhanoglu together until 2027|date=5 July 2023|access-date=17 August 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 6 July, Inter signed midfielder [[Davide Frattesi]] from [[US Sassuolo Calcio|Sassuolo]] on a season-long loan with an obligation to buy, for a reported total fee of €33 million.<ref name="Frattesi">{{cite news|url=https://gianlucadimarzio.com/it/inter-frattesi-dettagli-sassuolo-calciomercato-news|title=Inter, i dettagli dell'affare Frattesi con il Sassuolo: visite forse già venerdì|date=4 July 2023|access-date=6 July 2023|language=it|publisher=Gianluca Di Marzio}}</ref> On 7 July, Inter activated the option to make Acerbi transfer from [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] permanent.<ref name=acerbi>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/francesco-acerbi-signs-on-permanently|title=Francesco Acerbi signs on permanently|date=7 July 2023|access-date=7 July 2023|language=en|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> A day later, Inter also confirmed that De Vrij extended his contract with the club until 2025.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/stefan-de-vrij-inter-contract-extension-2025|title=Inter and Stefan De Vrij together until 2025|date=8 July 2023|access-date=17 August 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 12 July, Inter triggered the €7 million release clause to sign promising German centre-back [[Yann Aurel Bisseck]] from Danish club [[Aarhus Gymnastikforening|AGF]], who agreed to a five-year deal.<ref name=bisseck>{{cite news|url=https://www.calcioefinanza.it/2023/07/13/bisseck-stipendio-inter-quanto-guadagna/|title=Bisseck è un nuovo calciatore dell'Inter: l'impatto a bilancio|date=13 July 2023|access-date=12 November 2023|language=it|publisher=Calcio e Finanza}}</ref> On 13 July, Inter officialised the return of former [[Inter Milan Youth Sector|youth academy]] goalkeeper [[Raffaele Di Gennaro]].<ref name=digennaro>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/raffaele-di-gennaro-signs-for-inter|title=Raffaele Di Gennaro is a new Inter player|date=13 July 2023|access-date=13 July 2023|language=en|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 19 July, Colombian right-midfielder [[Juan Cuadrado]] joined Inter as a free agent in a rather controversial move, after his contract with bitter rivals [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] expired.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://football-italia.net/cuadrado-to-inter-ultras-protest-and-want-to-meet-ex-juventus-winger/|title=Cuadrado to Inter: Ultras protest and want to meet ex-Juventus winger|date=18 July 2023|access-date=12 November 2023|language=it|publisher=Football-Italia}}</ref> On 20 July, after weeks of speculations, goalkeeper [[André Onana]] joined [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] for a reported fee of €52.5 million, agreeing to a five-year contract with the English side and leaving Inter after only one season.<ref name=onana>{{cite news|url=https://sport.sky.it/calciomercato/2023/07/20/onana-saluto-inter-calciomercato-news|title=United, ufficiale Onana. Il saluto all'Inter: "Mi mancherai, proverò eterna gratitudine"|date=20 July 2023|access-date=20 July 2023|language=it|publisher=[[Sky Sport (Italy)]]}}</ref> On 26 July, [[Lautaro Martínez]] was officially appointed as the new club captain after the departures of former skipper Handanović and club veterans D'Ambrosio, Brozović and Škriniar.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.goal.com/it/liste/inter-lautaro-martinez-nuovo-capitano-nerazzurro/bltf16a2002874147ed|title=L'ufficialità dell'Inter: Lautaro Martinez è il nuovo capitano nerazzurro|date=26 July 2023|access-date=5 November 2023|language=it|publisher=[[Goal (website)|Goal]]}}</ref> On 7 August, Inter signed Swiss goalkeeper [[Yann Sommer]] from [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] for a reported €6.75 million fee, penning a three-year contract with the club.<ref name=sommer>{{cite news|url=https://www.calcioefinanza.it/2023/08/07/inter-sommer-impatto-bilancio/|title=Inter, arriva Sommer: l'impatto dell'operazione a bilancio|date=7 August 2023|access-date=9 August 2023|language=it|publisher=Calcio e Finanza}}</ref> Three days later, Inter completed their goalkeeper roster by signing [[Emil Audero]] from [[UC Sampdoria|Sampdoria]] on a season-long loan.<ref name=audero>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/emil-audero-new-inter-player|title=Emil Audero is a new Inter player |date=10 August 2023|access-date=10 August 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 15 August, [[Robin Gosens]] joined [[1. FC Union Berlin|Union Berlin]] on a permanent transfer for a reported fee of €15 million.<ref name=gosens>{{cite news|url=https://sport.sky.de/fussball/artikel/transfer-news-robin-gosens-bei-union-berlin-zum-medizincheck/12940624/35311|title=Gosens in Berlin gelandet! Medizincheck läuft|date=15 August 2023|access-date=15 August 2023|language=de|publisher=[[Sky Sport (Germany)]]}}</ref> On the same day, Inter filled the now-vacant left wing-back spot by signing [[Carlos Augusto (footballer)|Carlos Augusto]] from [[AC Monza|Monza]] on a season-long loan with a conditional obligation to buy for a reported total fee of €12 million plus bonuses.<ref name=carlos>{{cite news|url=https://sport.sky.it/calciomercato/2023/08/14/inter-carlos-augusto-calciomercato-news|title=Inter, Carlos Augusto ha firmato: si attende solo l'annuncio|date=14 August 2023|access-date=15 August 2023|language=it|publisher=[[Sky Sport (Italy)]]}}</ref> On 16 August, Austrian striker [[Marko Arnautović]] made his return to Inter after 13 years, joining from [[Bologna FC 1909|Bologna]] on a season-long loan with a conditional obligation to buy for a reported fee of €8 million.<ref name=arna>{{cite news|url=https://www.calcioefinanza.it/2023/08/14/arnautovic-stipendio-inter-bilancio/|title=Inter, arriva Arnautovic: l'impatto dell'operazione a bilancio|date=14 August 2023|access-date=16 August 2023|language=it|publisher=Calcio e Finanza}}</ref>

===August===
On 19 August, Inter started their [[2023–24 Serie A|Serie A]] campaign with a 2–0 home victory against [[AC Monza|Monza]], thanks to a brace from Lautaro.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni1intmon|title=INTER VS MONZA|date=19 August 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 25 August, [[Joaquín Correa]] joined French side [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] on a season-long loan with an option and a conditional obligation to buy.<ref name=correa>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/joaquin-correa-joins-olympique-de-marseille|title=Joaquín Correa joins Olympique de Marseille|date=25 August 2023|access-date=26 August 2023|language=en|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> The next day, [[Alexis Sánchez]] returned to Inter, joining as a free agent following his season-long experience at Marseille.<ref name=alexis>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/alexis-sanchez-new-inter-player|title=Alexis Sánchez is a new Inter player|date=26 August 2023|access-date=26 August 2023|language=en|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 28 August, Inter grabbed his second win by beating [[Cagliari Calcio|Cagliari]] 2–0 with the goals from [[Denzel Dumfries]] and Lautaro.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni2cagint|title=CAGLIARI VS INTER|date=28 August 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 30 August, after weeks of negotiations, Inter completed the signing of French defender [[Benjamin Pavard]] from Bayern Munich for a reported fee of €30 million, signing a five-year contract.<ref name=pavard>{{cite news|url=https://www.calcioefinanza.it/2023/08/22/pavard-stipendio-inter-quanto-guadagna/|title=Pavard all'Inter, ora ci siamo: l'impatto dell'operazione a bilancio|date=30 August 2023|access-date=30 August 2023|language=it|publisher=Calcio e Finanza}}</ref>

===September===
On the deadline day of the summer transfer window, Inter signed Dutch midfielder [[Davy Klaassen]] from [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] on a free transfer.<ref name=klaassen>{{cite news|url=https://onefootball.com/it/notizie/klaassen-la-formula-del-trasferimento-dallajax-allinter-dettagli-38151406|title=Klaassen, la formula del trasferimento dall'Ajax all'Inter. Dettagli|date=2 September 2023|access-date=3 September 2023|language=it|publisher=OneFootball}}</ref> On 3 September, Inter convincingly defeated [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] 4–0 thanks to the debut goal of Thuram, a brace from Lautaro and a penalty kick transformed by Çalhanoğlu.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni3intfio|title=INTER VS FIORENTINA|date=3 September 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 5 September, head coach [[Simone Inzaghi]] extended his contract with the ''Nerazzurri'' until 2025.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/simone-inzaghi-inter-contract-renewal-2025|title=Inter and Simone Inzaghi together until 2025|date=5 September 2023|access-date=6 December 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 16 September, the [[Derby della Madonnina]] took place, with both Inter and [[AC Milan|Milan]] undefeated and joint top of the table. In a dominating display, Inter beat their city rivals 5–1 thanks to a brace from [[Henrikh Mkhitaryan]], a goal from Thuram, a penalty transformed by Çalhanoğlu, and the debut goal of Frattesi, with [[Rafael Leão]] scoring the only goal for the ''Rossoneri''.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni4intmil|title=INTER VS MILAN|date=16 September 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 20 September, Inter struggled away from home against [[Real Sociedad]] in their seasonal [[2023–24 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] debut, with the Spanish side taking the lead early in the game thanks to [[Brais Méndez]], and Lautaro scoring a late equaliser to earn the ''Nerazzurri'' a 1–1 draw.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039184/|title=Real Sociedad-Inter|date=20 September 2023|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> Four days later, Inter continued their domestic league winning streak by defeating [[Empoli FC|Empoli]] 1–0, thanks to a half-volley goal from outside the box by [[Federico Dimarco]].<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni5empint|title=EMPOLI VS INTER|date=24 September 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 27 September, Inter suffered their first defeat of the season, losing 1–2 against [[US Sassuolo Calcio|Sassuolo]], with the team's only goal coming from Dumfries.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni6intsas|title=INTER VS SASSUOLO|date=27 September 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Three days later, the ''Nerazzurri'' returned to their winning ways by defeating [[US Salernitana 1919|Salernitana]] 4–0, with Lautaro scoring all four goals after being subbed on in the 55th minute.<ref name="salint">{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni7salint|title=SALERNITANA VS INTER|date=30 September 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref>

===October===
On 3 October, Inter faced [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] in the second match of the Champions League, defeating the Portuguese team 1–0 with a goal from Thuram.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039105/|title=Inter-Benfica|date=3 October 2023|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> Four days later, Inter drew 2–2 against [[Bologna FC 1909|Bologna]], blowing the 2–0 lead obtained with the goals from Acerbi and Lautaro.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni8intbol|title=INTER VS BOLOGNA|date=7 October 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 21 October, goals from Lautaro, Thuram, and a stoppage-time penalty kick scored by Çalhanoğlu granted Inter a 3–0 win against [[Torino FC|Torino]].<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni9torint|title=TORINO VS INTER|date=21 October 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Three days later, Inter won 2–1 against [[FC Red Bull Salzburg|Red Bull Salzburg]] in the third match of the Champions League, thanks to the goals from Sánchez and Çalhanoğlu.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039115/|title=Inter-Salzburg|date=24 October 2023|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> On 29 October, Inter defeated [[AS Roma|Roma]] 1–0, with Thuram breaking the deadlock in the 81st minute.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni10introm|title=INTER VS ROMA|date=29 October 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref>

===November===
Inter started the month of November by obtaining a 2–1 win against [[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]], with goals from Çalhanoğlu and Lautaro.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni11ataint|title=ATALANTA VS INTER|date=4 November 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 8 November, Inter managed to beat Salzburg away from home 1–0 courtesy of a late penalty kick scored by Lautaro, and advanced to the round of 16 of the Champions League with two games to spare.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039145/|title=Salzburg-Inter|date=8 November 2023|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> On 12 November, Inter defeated [[Frosinone Calcio|Frosinone]] 2–0, thanks to a 56 metres lob goal from Dimarco and a penalty kick scored by Çalhanoğlu.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni12intfro|title=INTER VS FROSINONE|date=12 November 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 26 November, Inter secured a 1–1 away draw in the [[Derby d'Italia]] against [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] to maintain the top spot of the league, with Lautaro scoring the equaliser after a [[Dušan Vlahović]] goal.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni13juvint|title=JUVENTUS VS INTER|date=26 November 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Three days later, the away match against Benfica in the Champions League ended 3–3, with the heavily rotated ''Nerazzurri'' side coming back in the second half with the goals from Arnautović, Frattesi, and Sánchez, after [[João Mário (footballer, born January 1993)|João Mário]] scored a first-half hat-trick against his former club.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039160/|title=Benfica-Inter|date=29 November 2023|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref>

===December===
On 3 December, Inter started the new month by defeating reigning Serie A champions [[SSC Napoli|Napoli]] 3–0 at the [[Stadio Diego Armando Maradona]], with goals from Hakan Çalhanoğlu, [[Nicolò Barella]] and Marcus Thurman.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni14napint|title=NAPOLI VS INTER|date=3 December 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 9 December, the ''Nerazzurri'' comfortably won against [[Udinese Calcio|Udinese]] 4–0, thanks to the goals from Çalhanoğlu, Dimarco, Thuram and Lautaro.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni15intudi|title=INTER VS UDINESE|date=9 December 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Three days later, the last match of the Champions League group stage against Real Sociedad ended in a goalless draw, with the Basque side qualifying for the round of 16 as group winners thanks to the better goal difference and Inter finishing second place.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039168/|title=Inter-Real Sociedad|date=12 December 2023|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> On 17 December, Inter defeated [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] 2–0 away from home, courtesy of the goals from Lautaro and Thuram.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni16lazint|title=LAZIO VS INTER|date=17 December 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On the same day it was officially announced that Inter was qualified for the [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup]] due to their  4-year UEFA ranking.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/inter-club-world-cup-2025|title=Inter to take part in the Club World Cup 2025: the details|date=17 December 2023|language=en|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> On 20 December, a headed goal from Carlos Augusto in the extra-time was not enough to defeat Bologna in the round of 16 of the [[2023–24 Coppa Italia|Coppa Italia]] and Inter were beaten by a scoreline of 1–2, knocking them out of the competition.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24cpitaottavuni1intbol|title=INTER VS BOLOGNA|date=20 December 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 23 December, Bisseck's debut goal and a strike from Barella gave the ''Nerazzurri'' a 2–0 home victory over [[US Lecce|Lecce]].<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni17intlec|title=INTER VS LECCE|date=23 December 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 29 December, Inzaghi's team drew 1–1 against [[Genoa CFC|Genoa]] away from home in their last match of the year, with the only goal for Inter coming from Marko Arnautović.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni18genint|title=GENOA VS INTER|date=29 December 2023|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 30 December, the club announced the contract extension for three players: Federico Dimarco until 2027,<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/federico-dimarco-extends-inter-contract-2027|title=Federico Dimarco extends Inter contract until 2027|date=30 December 2023|access-date=30 December 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> [[Matteo Darmian]] until 2025,<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/darmian-renewal-inter-2025|title=Inter and Matteo Darmian together until 2025|date=30 December 2023|access-date=30 December 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> and Henrikh Mkhitaryan until 2026.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/henrikh-mkhitaryan-inter-renewal-2026|title=Inter and Henrikh Mkhitaryan together until 2026|date=30 December 2023|access-date=30 December 2023|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref>

===January===
On 5 January, following Juan Cuadrado's [[Achilles tendon]] surgery,<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/juan-cuadrado-condition-injury|title=Juan Cuadrado's condition|date=20 December 2023|access-date=5 January 2024|publisher=Inter Milan}}</ref> Inter signed Canadian wing-back [[Tajon Buchanan]] from [[Club Brugge KV|Club Brugge]] as his replacement for a fee of €8.5 million plus bonuses.<ref name="Buchanan">{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5181802/2024/01/05/tajon-buchanan-inter-milan-canada/|title=Inside Tajon Buchanan's transfer to Inter Milan, and what it means for Canada|date=5 January 2024|access-date=6 January 2024|publisher=[[The Athletic]]}}</ref> The following day Inter beat [[Hellas Verona FC|Verona]] 2–1 in the first match of the year thanks to the goals from Lautaro and Frattesi, ending the first half of the season as league leaders.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni19intver|title=INTER VS HELLAS VERONA|date=6 January 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 13 January, Inzaghi's side won 5–1 against Monza away from home, thanks to the braces from Çalhanoğlu and Lautaro, and a goal from Thuram.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni20monint|title=MONZA VS INTER|date=13 January 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> After the game against Monza, Inter flew to [[Riyadh]] in [[Saudi Arabia]] to play the [[2023 Supercoppa Italiana|Supercoppa Italiana]]. As a result, the match against Atalanta originally scheduled for 21 January was postponed,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.calcioefinanza.it/2023/12/22/serie-a-date-rinvii-supercoppa/|title=Serie A, decise le date dei rinvii per le squadre che giocano in Supercoppa|date=22 December 2023|access-date=17 February 2024|language=it|publisher=Calcio e Finanza}}</ref> and Juventus surpassed the ''Nerazzurri'' as league leaders by defeating Lecce on 22 January and having a game in hand.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.ansa.it/sito/notizie/sport/calcio/2024/01/21/la-juventus-vince-3-0-a-lecce-sorpasso-nel-segno-di-vlahovic_8400a412-ccdc-4511-83b7-fba1581b2515.html|title=La Juventus vince 3-0 a Lecce, sorpasso nel segno di Vlahovic|date=22 January 2024|access-date=17 February 2024|language=it|publisher=[[Agenzia Nazionale Stampa Associata|ANSA]]}}</ref> On 19 January, Inter defeated Lazio 3–0 in the semi-finals of the Supercoppa Italiana with the goals from Thuram, Çalhanoğlu, and Frattesi.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24scitasemifuni1intlaz|title=INTER VS LAZIO|date=19 January 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Three days later the ''Nerazzurri'' beat Napoli 1–0 in the final thanks to an injury time strike from Lautaro Martínez, winning their eighth Supercoppa title, the third in a row.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24scitafinaluni1napint|title=NAPOLI VS INTER|date=22 January 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 28 January, a header from Lautaro secured Inter a 1–0 victory over Fiorentina in the last match of the month,<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni22fioint|title=FIORENTINA VS INTER|date=28 January 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> and allowed the team to regain the top spot of the league, after Juventus drew against Empoli.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni22juvemp|title=JUVENTUS VS EMPOLI|date=27 January 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref>

===February===
On 4 February, the second Derby d'Italia of the season was decided by an own goal from [[Federico Gatti]], granting Inter a 1–0 victory at San Siro and increasing the point gap between the two teams.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni23intjuv|title=INTER VS JUVENTUS|date=4 February 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 10 February, Inter obtained a 4–2 comeback win against Roma at the [[Stadio Olimpico]] with goals from Acerbi, Thuram and Bastoni, as well as an own goal from [[Angeliño]].<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni24romint|title=ROMA VS INTER|date=10 February 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 16 February, goals from Thuram, Lautaro, Dumfries and Arnautović granted Inter a comfortable 4–0 home victory over Salernitana.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni25intsal|title=INTER VS SALERNITANA|date=16 February 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Four days later, Inzaghi's side obtained a tight 1–0 win in the first leg of the Champions League round of 16 against [[Atlético Madrid]], with Marko Arnautović claiming the winning goal.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039918/|title=Inter-Atlético de Madrid|date=20 February 2024|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> On 25 February, a brace from Lautaro and the goals from Frattesi and De Vrij gave Inter a 4–0 win against Lecce.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni26lecint|title=LECCE VS INTER|date=25 February 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Three days later, Inter defeated Atalanta 4–0 in the last match of the month, thanks to the goals from Darmian, Lautaro, Dimarco and Frattesi. This extended the gap between Inter Milan and 2nd placed Juventus by 12 points.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni21intata|title=INTER VS ATALANTA|date=28 February 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref>

===March===
On 4 March, [[Kristjan Asllani]]'s first goal with the ''Nerazzurri'' shirt and a penalty kick scored by Alexis Sánchez granted Inter a narrow 2–1 win against Genoa, widening the gap between Inzaghi's side and Juventus by 15 points.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni27intgen|title=INTER VS GENOA|date=4 March 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Five days later, Inter won their tenth consecutive league game by beating Bologna 1–0 through a Yann Bisseck header.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni28bolint|title=BOLOGNA VS INTER|date=9 March 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 13 March, Inter's campaign in the UEFA Champions League came to an end in the round of 16, after going out on penalties to Atlético Madrid. Inter initially took the lead with a Federico Dimarco goal, but the ''Colchoneros'' came back with strikes from [[Antoine Griezmann]] and [[Memphis Depay]], setting the aggregate score to 2–2, before eliminating the ''Nerazzurri'' in the penalty shoot-out. This was Inter's first defeat in 2024, interrupting a 13-match winning streak in all competitions.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028b-1a6716f1730a-879d57ef7026-1000--report-atletico-beat-inter-on-penalties/|title=Atlético de Madrid 2-1 Inter (agg: 2-2 aet, 3-2 pens): Atleti edge thriller in dramatic shoot-out|date=13 March 2024|language=en|publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> On 17 March, Inter drew 1–1 against Napoli in Serie A, with the only goal for the Black and Blues coming from Darmian.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni29intnap|title=INTER VS NAPOLI|date=17 March 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref>

===April===
On the first match of the month against Empoli on 1 April, Inter returned to their winning ways by defeating the Tuscanian team 2–0, thanks to the goals from Federico Dimarco and Alexis Sánchez.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni30intemp|title=INTER VS EMPOLI|date=1 April 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On the same day, also as a result of the goalless draw between Lecce and Roma, Inter was the first Italian team to qualify for the [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.goal.com/it/liste/inter-matematicamente-qualificata-alla-prossima-champions-league/blt2f24c0cd80f28d75#cs9b091913505a97a9|title=Arriva il primo verdetto: Inter matematicamente qualificata alla prossima Champions League|date=1 April 2024|access-date=17 April 2024|language=it|publisher=Goal.com}}</ref> On 8 April, a penalty kick from Çalhanoğlu and an injury time goal from Frattesi granted Inter a 2–1 comeback win against Udinese.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni31udiint|title=UDINESE VS INTER|date=8 April 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 14 April, goals from Thuram and Çalhanoğlu were not enough to beat Cagliari at San Siro, with the game ending in a 2–2 draw.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni32intcag|title=INTER VS CAGLIARI|date=14 April 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 22 April, Inter beat Milan 2–1 in the second Derby della Madonnina of the season thanks to the goals from Acerbi and Thuram, mathematically securing their historic 20th ''[[scudetto]]'' and second [[Star (sport badge)|star]] with five games to spare.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni33milint|title=MILAN VS INTER|date=22 April 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> Six days later, a brace from Çalhanoğlu gave Inter a 2–0 win against Torino in the last match of April.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni34inttor|title=INTER VS TORINO|date=28 April 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref>

===May===
Inter started May by suffering their second league loss of the season, losing 0–1 against relegation-battling Sassuolo, with the ''Neroverdi'' defeating the newly crowned champions again after doing so in the first half of the campaign and ending their 28-game unbeaten run in Serie A.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni35sasint|title=SASSUOLO VS INTER|date=4 May 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 10 May, Inter returned to winning ways by defeating Frosinone 5–0 with the goals from Frattesi, Arnautović, Buchanan (his debut goal with the ''Nerazzurri'' shirt), Lautaro and Thuram.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni36froint|title=FROSINONE VS INTER|date=10 May 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 19 May, Inter drew 1–1 against Lazio in the last home game of the season, with Dumfries scoring the late equaliser.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni37intlaz|title=INTER VS LAZIO|date=19 May 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> On 26 May, Inter drew 2–2 against Verona in the last match of the season, with Marko Arnautović scoring a brace for the ''Nerazzurri''.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.legaseriea.it/en/match/2023-24aunicouni38verint|title=HELLAS VERONA VS INTER|date=26 May 2024|language=en|publisher=Lega Serie A}}</ref> At the end of the season Lautaro Martínez finished as [[Capocannoniere|league's top scorer]] with 24 goals,<ref name=Lautarotopscorer/> and was named [[Serie A Awards#2023–24|Serie A MVP]] of the season.<ref name=LautaroMVP/> Alessandro Bastoni and Hakan Çalhanoğlu won the best Serie A defender and midfielder awards of the season respectively,<ref name=MVPs/> and Simone Inzaghi was named the best coach of the league for 2023–24.<ref name=InzaghiMVP/>

